course: dictionary
lesson: 3712
title: One word, many meanings: flesh
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. «Flesh» is a good example. In the first meaning, flesh is the soft part of a person's or animal's body, between the skin and the bones. In the second, it is the soft part of a fruit or vegetable that you can eat.
explain: Read the words around the word to pick the right meaning. «Cut an apple for your lunchbox and by break time the white flesh has gone brown.» The apple is cut and eaten, so flesh here means the soft part inside the fruit, not the human body.
explain: Writers also use flesh as a verb. To flesh out an idea or a story means to add more details, so the idea gets fuller and easier to picture. Teachers often ask pupils to flesh out their characters.
fact: Flesh is a noun, and it is uncountable, so we say some flesh, not a flesh.
fact: «Flesh» means the soft part of a body between the skin and bones, or the soft edible part of a fruit or vegetable.
fact: «To flesh out» means to add more details to an idea or a story.
fact: «In the flesh» means in person, for real, not on a screen or in a photo.
fact: In old church language, «the Word became flesh» means that Christians believe God became a human in Jesus.
exercise: «Cut an apple for your lunchbox and by break time the white flesh has gone brown.» What does flesh mean here? | *The soft part inside the fruit that you can eat | The seeds in the middle | The paper bag it was kept in | The skin of the apple
exercise: «The Earth is built in layers, a bit like a peach with a thin skin, thick flesh and a hard stone in the middle.» Which word names the thick, soft middle part? | *flesh | layers | skin | stone
exercise: Mrs Patel says, «Please flesh out your character before you start writing.» What does she want? | *To add more details about the character | To write the character very quietly | To forget the character | To cut the character into pieces
test: «The actor was even better in the flesh than on the video.» What does «in the flesh» mean here? | Very quietly | Wearing a costume | Covered in mud | *In person, for real
test: «The bones are covered by muscle and fat, which together make up the flesh.» What is the flesh in this sentence? | The hard part of the body | The bones of the body | The skin only | *The soft part of the body between the skin and bones
exam: Ravi says flesh always means the inside of a fruit. Which sentence proves him wrong? | *«The doctor said the cut had burned the flesh of his hand.» | «She fleshed out her plan for the school show.» | «The Earth has a flesh-like mantle of hot rock.» | «The flesh of a peach is juicy.»
